<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Viral upper respiratory infection (J06.9)</content>. A common cold. Rapid tests for influenza and COVID-19 are negative. No features of bacterial sinusitis or pneumonia, so no antibiotic, in line with CDC advice. [S]<list><item>Saline nasal spray, warm fluids and honey for the cough.</item><item>Acetaminophen as needed, no more than 3,000 mg a day; no ibuprofen or naproxen while his blood pressure is untreated.</item><item>Return for shortness of breath, chest pain, a fever above 38.5 °C, or symptoms lasting more than ten days or worsening after improving.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Cocaine use disorder with cocaine-induced psychotic disorder, on aripiprazole (F14.250)</content>. No decongestants: pseudoephedrine and phenylephrine raise blood pressure, which is already high, act as stimulants that can stir cravings, and pseudoephedrine can make a urine screen read falsely positive for amphetamine. Combination cold products also often contain them. Cough medicines with dextromethorphan are best avoided too. Persecutory fears easing on aripiprazole. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or of harming anyone. [S]<list><item>Continue aripiprazole 5 mg each morning.</item><item>Show the pharmacist this list before buying any cold medicine.</item><item>Continue the contingency management urine tests.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Raised blood pressure (R03.0)</content>. 146/92 mmHg with a mild fever; a diagnosis waits for the home readings planned for the four-week visit. [S]<list><item>Home readings twice daily in the week before the four-week visit, as planned.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The ASAM/AAAP Clinical Practice Guideline on the Management of Stimulant Use Disorder (2023); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); Outpatient Clinical Care for Adults (antibiotic use) (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Four-week review in one week. Return sooner as above. Next scheduled visit 2026-08-10: four-week review: psychotic symptoms, urine tests, blood pressure and a diagnosis of hypertension. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
